t\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=2\\ne 3={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=1.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-2,0]$ is allowed.  The intervals $(1,3]$ and $[-2,1)$ are not closed, $[-2,1]$ and $[0,3]$ contain the point $x=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398835 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398835 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398835 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398835 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398835 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398835 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398835 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398835 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186398836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398835 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b49fc3fc-44cb-5db2-9fc5-ed9e471c021f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398836 #edb/ref 1080 "[-2,0]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398836 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398836 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5ff46e30-81e1-52bf-b55f-724406f58c7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398837 #edb/ref 1080 "[-2,1]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398837 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398837 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9c6c153e-3504-5702-bfa8-a1175ee38323" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398838 #edb/ref 1080 "(1,3]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398838 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398838 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "586fee85-5bd1-5bd9-b903-3f988c962fea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398839 #edb/ref 1080 "[0,3]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398839 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398839 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "89bd78d0-e4f8-53a4-8ab8-3044e809bf86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398840 #edb/ref 1080 "[-2,1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398840 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398840 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7b8bb4af-dfd9-5eef-baf3-2724ee4fd9e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399461 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399461 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105248" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399461 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399461 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine if the vector $u$ lies on the plane $Π:r=sb+tc$, $t,s\\in R$, where  $b=⟨1,-4,2⟩$, $c=⟨3,-10,2⟩$, and  $u=⟨-2,5,0⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $u=sb+tc.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399461 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da64bb93-914e-5229-bb6d-b7d97e6fa1bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399461 #edb/ref 1074 "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s+3t=-2 \\\\ -4s-10t=5 \\\\ 2s+2t=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -10 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=-2,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of $s$ and $t$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc=u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $u$ does *not* lie on the plane $Π.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399462 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399462 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399462 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399462 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399462 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399462 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399462 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399462 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186399467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399462 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3ded2025-f849-5e24-ae50-154d00e66f84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399463 #edb/ref 1080 "12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399463 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399463 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5469c2f4-10d4-551a-8387-1f41e195f9f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399464 #edb/ref 1080 "-9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399464 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399464 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b5aaf75e-0319-5e2f-b6a1-406f9f457622" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399465 #edb/ref 1080 "-18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399465 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399465 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6f0f386a-54e4-5bbd-999d-c5b1e894fea2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399466 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399466 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399466 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ea345608-0f4c-5f8a-9cea-a53c46ba68a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399467 #edb/ref 1080 "$u$ does not lie on $Π$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399467 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399467 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f370dd07-5630-5589-a9df-b8eef801b693" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399538 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399538 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106775" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399538 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399538 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399538 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56a4f3a9-ccca-53d2-8026-09ec5330fdbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399538 #edb/ref 1074 "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}16=2\\lambda  \\\\ -8=-4\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399539 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399539 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399539 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399539 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399539 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399539 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/r